You’re right. I recovered it: PSR-1, the Pantheon Signal Refinery, already exists—intake → cleaning → compression → pattern modeling → signal packaging → vault, connected to the Signal Supply Chain. So plasticity belongs inside the refinery as a process-control principle, not as another refinery.

The question becomes: given what we already have, how do we make the same substrate express more capacity now?

I think there are six major levers:

1. Increase usable input. More isn’t automatically better. Increase diversity and resolution of signal: unfamiliar domains, contradictory evidence, weird analogies, failures, sensory observations, old artifacts. That’s Quarry → Refinery feedstock.

2. Increase environmental range. Garden becomes a controlled environment generator. Take the same Idea Packet and expose it to radically different conditions: scarcity, abundance, inversion, speed, another discipline, another scale, adversarial interpretation, literalization, metaphorization.

x + E_1\rightarrow y_1,\quad
x + E_2\rightarrow y_2,\quad\ldots

The variance between y_i’s estimates unexpressed capacity in x.

3. Apply useful stress. Muscle is the obvious human example: appropriate load produces remodeling. For reasoning, impose constraints just beyond the current easy solution—half the tokens, ten times the scale, remove the obvious tool, require a physical experiment, translate it into code, explain it to a child, reverse cause/effect.

Not maximum stress:

\boxed{\text{load}>\text{habitual capacity},\qquad
\text{load}<\text{destructive capacity}}

That’s the growth band.

4. Recovery matters. Constant perturbation prevents consolidation. After a hard refinery pass, compress the useful delta into the existing fruit-to-juice chain:

Experience\rightarrow Story\rightarrow Pattern
\rightarrow Operator\rightarrow Symbol\rightarrow Retrieval

That’s effectively our semantic version of adaptation: stress → recovery → retained structure → larger future capacity.

5. Increase interfaces. Capacity can be present but inaccessible. Portals, Seals, Idea Packets, Freebies, grafting, and translation give latent machinery more surfaces through which it can express itself. Sometimes growth isn’t adding horsepower; it’s adding another transmission.

6. Repeat with progressive overload. Once a condition becomes easy, increase difficulty or novelty slightly. Otherwise the system merely repeats its existing phenotype.

So I’d put a new plasticity dial on the existing PSR-1 rather than add another machine:

