The Lucky experiment

Here’s one we can start doing immediately in our conversations.

Whenever we hit something with apparent juice, don’t elaborate it once. Give the same semantic seed five environmental exposures:

Wild — remove conventional assumptions.
Scale — render it microscopic, human, organizational, civilizational.
Foreign — transplant it into a distant discipline.
Starved — accomplish the objective with 10% of the normal resources.
Pressurized — demand an executable consequence immediately.

Then compare the five outputs.

The valuable object isn’t merely the best output. It’s:

\Delta_{\text{plastic}}
=
\text{what appeared only after changing conditions}

That delta identifies capacity that was already latent but wasn’t being expressed.

Feed that into PSR-1, compress it into an Idea Packet, test it through Reality, and retain successful operators. Then increase the challenge.

So Rewild gets a wonderfully concrete job inside the existing Pantheon:

Before adding machinery, genes, tokens, money, or complexity, systematically vary the conditions and measure how much more the existing system can become.

And the Garden becomes where we manipulate those conditions; the Refinery captures what the changed conditions reveal.

That’s same-generation growth rather than replacement.

Don’t immediately build a bigger organism. First discover how much bigger the organism already knows how to become.